<QuestionLayout
  title="Does the same Traverse WASM give byte-identical output on every host?"
  description="Same WASM, same bytes in, same bytes out. On v0.15.0 hosts don't always pass the same bytes in: traverse-mcp re-sorts JSON keys, so core.authorize's policy_hash differs. What matches, what doesn't, and traverse#1722."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/does-the-same-wasm-give-byte-identical-output-on-every-host.html"
  category="Platforms and portability"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the guarantee is "same WASM, same input bytes, same output bytes." The part that isn't nailed down yet on v0.15.0 is the middle: different hosts can turn the same logical request into different bytes before the capability sees it. When that happens, any capability that hashes, signs or echoes its input will differ across hosts, even though its logic didn't change.</p>

  <h2>Where we saw it</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/agent-mcp-real-server.html">October 23 weekly demo</a> ran one catalog capability, <code>core.authorize@1.2.0</code> (<code>sha256:0c318fff…</code>), on three hosts with the same logical input:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the browser, through <code>traverse-embedder-web</code> 0.15.0 and <code>runtime.wasm</code>;</li>
    <li>Node, through the same npm package;</li>
    <li><code>traverse-mcp</code> 0.15.0 in Mode A, on native wasmtime.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Decision, reason, obligations and trace matched on all three. <code>policy_hash</code> didn't: <code>fnv1a64:2a126b86…</code> on both embedders, <code>fnv1a64:51a64653…</code> on <code>traverse-mcp</code>.</p>

  <h2>Why</h2>
  <p><code>core.authorize</code> hashes the raw policy bytes it receives. <code>traverse-mcp</code> parses the request into a JSON value and writes it back out with keys sorted. The embedders pass the caller's JSON through untouched. So the WASM was fed two different byte strings that mean the same thing, and it hashed what it was given. Sending the embedders sorted-key input made all three hosts byte-identical, and hashing the policy in each key order reproduced each value. The capability was deterministic the whole time. The hosts weren't agreeing on its input.</p>

  <h2>What this means for "works on my machine"</h2>
  <p>Traverse moves business logic into one sandboxed artifact so it can't quietly fork per platform. That holds: the logic can't see the OS, and the WASM bytes were identical on every host. But the boundary between host and capability is part of the contract too. If one host canonicalizes input and another doesn't, you get a "works on my host" bug that lives outside the capability. The other known example is model evidence: hosts currently hand-pick <code>ModelRightsRecord</code> fields for <code>model.execute</code>, which can also produce different bytes for the same model (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/641">registry#641</a>, open).</p>

  <h2>What to do on v0.15.0</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compare the fields that carry meaning.</strong> For <code>core.authorize</code>, decision, reason, obligations and trace are stable across hosts. Don't compare <code>policy_hash</code> across different host types yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Canonicalize on the caller side</strong> if you need byte-identical output across hosts today. Sorted-key JSON was enough to make the demo's three hosts agree.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't hash raw input in new capabilities</strong> unless you canonicalize it first inside the capability.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What's open</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1722">traverse#1722</a> asks for one input-bytes rule for every host, either canonical JSON everywhere or byte-preserving pass-through everywhere, stated in the spec, plus a conformance fixture (a capability that returns the sha256 of its stdin) that has to agree on browser, Node, the native runtime and <code>traverse-mcp</code> in CI. Neither the decision nor the fixture has landed. Until it does, "same output on every host" on this site means same logic and same WASM bytes, with this input-serialization caveat.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
